# Timber - ten woodwinds, each in its own range. The reed is honest now; the
# timbres are still as seeded.
#
# **Every patch says the range it is played in, and that was most of what
# was wrong with it.** `body` is the instrument's lowest note, and the bodies
# here are the real instruments' bottom notes - the seeds had that right. But
# the `note=` centres had been placed by sweeping for "where it makes a
# pitch", and four of them (Alto Sax, Oboe, Stopped Wood, Forked) were
# *below* the instrument's own bottom note, with the audition phrase then
# going another octave down. Below the body the tube under the fingers
# clamps to nothing and what is left is a bare line and a reed - which is
# what was being heard as "breath and no note" and "the low ones are way
# out". So every patch now carries `range=lo..hi`, from the instrument and
# not from the model, the phrase stays inside it, and the app puts the
# keyboard there when the patch is loaded.
#
# **Three faults in the reed, all measured with `--track` (pitch against
# time, which the old scratch measurement could not do within forty cents):**
#
#   - The tongue was *opening* the reed. It scaled the whole table, rest and
#     all, so for twenty milliseconds a third of the mouth pressure flowed
#     into the tube, and the release shut it by a quarter: a thump into the
#     bore that every note started from. Clarinet read +50 cents at 30 ms
#     and settled by 60; now it is within 8 cents from 35 ms, before the
#     note is a tenth of its level. The tongue now holds the reed shut.
#   - Every cone had a mode under its bottom note. A cone's loop closes a
#     turn at DC, and the DC blocker's phase lead closed one again at ~40 Hz
#     with the reed's static gain still over one: 1.03 at the oboe's centre,
#     1.06 four semitones up, at which point it won and the oboe made no
#     pitch at all above note 64. A high-pass at a third of the bottom note
#     starves it (0.83 at the centre) and the oboe plays to 78.
#   - Nothing in the tube lost more at the top than the bottom, so every
#     mode sat within a few percent of the same gain: the bottom note of the
#     default instrument lost the race to its own third partial and the
#     bassoon had four modes over unity fighting through its attack. A wall
#     loss - a fifth off above four times the bottom note, the root law -
#     puts the fundamental ahead by the few percent a real bore does.
#
# And a reused voice no longer starts with the last note in its tube: the
# envelope is zeroed at note-on anyway, so clearing the pipe there is free.
#
# **The click on the front of every note was the pads.** Dan heard it on
# every instrument; measured as the largest second difference at a note-on
# against the fifty milliseconds after it, it was 5x to 13x, and with
# `keys` at zero 0.5x. The pad noise was a tenth of full scale at these
# settings - a four-millisecond burst at -25 dB on notes that take fifty to
# two hundred milliseconds to get there - so the engine scales it by a
# tenth (Timber.cpp, kKeyLevel) and the bank's values stand. Under that were
# two more: a note struck while the last one was still releasing cut it to
# nothing in one sample (it fades for two milliseconds now and starts at
# the next block), and the reed's rest was worked from each sample's breath
# against a slope solved once a block, which for the first block of a note
# swept it from the embouchure to shut and sent half the breath down the
# tube as a pulse (the rest is the solved one now). All nine patches read
# under 2x.
#
# Pitch at each centre now: Clarinet -0c, Bass Clarinet -2c, Alto Sax +2c,
# Oboe +2c, Bassoon +3c, Flute +2c, Pan Pipe +1c, Stopped Wood -4c, Forked
# +1c; the clarinet reads -1 / 0 / +5 at the bottom, middle and top of its
# range, and Stopped Wood is within five cents from 46 to 68. Levelled to
# -23 dB RMS, spread 2.6 dB without Init.
#
# **What is still wrong, in the order it matters.** The reeds are slow to
# speak on the big instruments - Bassoon 250 ms to -31 dB, Alto Sax and
# Oboe ~110 - because the loop gain is capped by the reed's own closure:
# `F = 2 r_rest - offset`, so at these embouchures the reed sits at 95 %
# closed for a gain of only 1.16-1.22, and Pipe::lift cannot raise it. That
# is also why `pressure` does nothing to the gain once at the cap. The fix
# is structural - the slope should be the reed's and the gain should follow
# from the pressure - and it is the next pass. The oboe still makes no
# pitch from note 80, its gain there 0.91, because the first-order lattice
# loses 11 % at half its cutoff where a real lattice loses nothing. The
# radiated fundamental sits 14 dB under its partials by construction
# (`arrive - 0.8 low`). And none of these yet sound like the instruments on
# the labels: the reed, embouchure, lattice and hole depth are all still as
# seeded, and that cannot be done from a table.
#
# Values are in each parameter's own units; `audition params Timber` lists
# them. A patch names only what it changes.

machine Timber
# A melody rather than one held note: one note is what one note sounds
# like, and a bank can pass that patch by patch and still be the same
# sound all the way down.
role    tune

patch Init

patch Clarinet  note=62 range=52..76
  attack         0.02 s
  breath         0.1
  keys           0.2

  volume         1.05
patch "Bass Clarinet"  note=50 range=40..62
  body           73.4 Hz
  breath         0.16
  embouchure     0.5
  keys           0.35
  lattice        1000 Hz
  pressure       0.9
  tongue         0.6

  volume         1.12
patch "Alto Sax"  note=60 range=50..74
  attack         0.015 s
  body           138.6 Hz
  bore           #1
  breath         0.2
  embouchure     0.5
  keys           0.3
  lattice        900 Hz
  pressure       0.95
  tongue         0.75
  vibrato        9 cents

  volume         1.03
patch Oboe  note=70 range=60..84
  attack         0.012 s
  body           233.1 Hz
  bore           #1
  breath         0.08
  embouchure     0.7
  family         #1
  keys           0.2
  lattice        1600 Hz
  pressure       0.95
  tongue         0.8
  vibrato        12 cents

  volume         1.31
patch Bassoon  note=46 range=36..60
  attack         0.025 s
  body           58.3 Hz
  bore           #1
  embouchure     0.6
  family         #1
  keys           0.4
  lattice        700 Hz
  pressure       0.9

  volume         1.24
patch Flute  note=72 range=62..86
  attack         0.04 s
  body           261.6 Hz
  bore           #1
  breath         0.45
  family         #2
  keys           0.15
  lattice        1400 Hz
  pressure       0.8
  tongue         0.35
  vibrato        10.8 cents

  volume         0.35
patch "Pan Pipe"  note=67 range=57..79
  aim            0.1
  attack         0.06 s
  body           196 Hz
  breath         0.6
  family         #2
  jet            0.55
  lattice        2200 Hz
  pressure       0.7
  tongue         0.2

  volume         0.71
patch "Stopped Wood"  note=55 range=46..68
  answer         0.385
  attack         0.05 s
  body           110 Hz
  breath         0.3
  # Dark from the filter now, not from the lattice. A lattice at 420 Hz on
  # a 110 Hz tube is under four times its bottom note - no real woodwind is
  # under six - and with the deep holes it made no note at all from 58 to
  # 64: measured, +18 and +222 cents at -37 dB, breath and nothing else,
  # which is what Dan heard in the top of the phrase. At 700 Hz with plain
  # holes it plays every note from 46 to 68 within five cents.
  cutoff         4000 Hz
  drive          0.2
  embouchure     0.45
  holes          0.5
  lattice        700 Hz
  pressure       1
  tongue         0.5

  volume         0.5
patch Forked  note=62 range=52..74
  # 0.4675, seeded from what shipped, is past the point where the fork
  # stops being a colour and becomes a note the tube cannot hold: it read
  # -4137 cents, which is no pitch at all. It plays at 0.32.
  answer         0.32
  attack         0.04 s
  below          1.74
  bore           #1
  breath         0.25
  embouchure     0.5
  family         #1
  fingering      0.8
  flutter        0.35
  lattice        1200 Hz
  pressure       1
  tongue         0.6

  volume         0.77